{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfVEzX8Zyzubzzk99CZT5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 12 is done and recorded. Nothing further is needed from tools; item 13 remains for the next iteration, so no `LOOP_COMPLETE` line was added.\n\n**Outcome.** Both open tree-decomposition theorems in `lean/MOSPFormalization/Sandwich.lean` are now proved without `sorry`, and `lake build` passes. The development's `sorry` count went from three to one. The one left is the fitted conjecture, kept as a statement by design.\n\n**What was proved.**\n- `treewidth_le_pathwidth`: a path decomposition is read as a tree decomposition on Mathlib's `pathGraph`. Mathlib proves that graph connected but not acyclic, so I proved `pathGraph_isTree` via the bridge characterisation, and the interval property gives the connectivity condition. The MOSP corollary `treewidth + 1 ≤ mospValue` is stated over the MOSP graph.\n- `branch_lemma`: three pairwise disjoint connected branches of pathwidth at least k, each attached to a vertex outside them, force pathwidth at least k + 1. The proof is the interval argument from Fellows and Langston 1987 and Kinnersley 1992. A treewidth form is derived from it.\n\n**The substantive finding.** The branch lemma as previously stated was false. It required neither connectivity nor attachment, and four isolated vertices with k = 0 satisfy every hypothesis while having pathwidth 0. Lean now holds a proof of that counterexample beside the corrected lemma. The corrected lemma also drops the old no-edges-between-branches hypotheses, which are unnecessary. Item 11's measurements are unaffected, since its code implemented the correct component rule.\n\n**Files touched.** `Sandwich.lean` (579 to 906 lines, 17 new declarations), `learning/sandwich.py` (inventory 17 proved / 3 stated to 28 / 1), `tests/test_sandwich.py` (two tests added), `reports/ml_nature.md` §39, `reports/sandwich_tables.md` regener